With this change, UPDATE becomes less strict, even in SERIALIZABLE mode (the default).

Example: `UPDATE t SET has_child = true WHERE id = 1 AND EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM t WHERE parent = 1)`. The UPDATE reads the child row through the subquery, but does not change it. If another job deletes the child row at the same time, the old check fails the UPDATE. The new check does not, so the UPDATE commits even though the child is gone.

