This logic is only correct if `shouldReturnColumnStats` is true.

By default, stats are not copied. There are two ways to produce stats from the scan:
- `Scan.includeColumnStats()` returns all stats
- `Scan.includeColumnStats(Collection<Integer>)` returns stats for specific field IDs

Both of those methods set `shouldReturnColumnStats(true)` on the context, but the second one also sets `columnsToKeepStats(Set<Integer>)`. That means `columnsToKeepStats()` is more specific than `returnColumnStats()`.

The correct logic is:
- If `columnsToKeepStats()` is non-null, use that set
- Otherwise, if `returnColumnStats()` is true, pass `TypeUtil.getProjectedIds(tableSchema())`
- Otherwise, do not call `projectStats`

That means the correctness of this method is dependent on how it is called. A better solution is to use the `Set<Integer>` argument as @amogh-jahagirdar suggests and embed this logic above:

```java

    if (shouldReturnColumnStats()) {
      if (columnsToKeepStats() != null) {
        planner.projectStats(columnsToKeepStats());
      } else {
        planner.projectStats(TypeUtil.getProjectedIds(tableSchema()));
      }
    }
```

